---
/**
 * 뉴스레터 전용 페이지.
 *
 * 폼 하나만 있는 페이지를 만들지 않는다. **무엇을 보내는지 먼저 말한다.**
 * 금융은 YMYL 이라 「이메일 주세요」만 있는 화면은 그 자체로 신뢰를 깎는다.
 * 발행 주체·주기·해지 방법·개인정보 취급을 한 화면에서 다 읽을 수 있어야 한다.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Newsletter from '../components/Newsletter.astro';
import { SITE_URL, PUBLISHER } from '../consts';
import stats from '../data/research-stats.json';

const TITLE = 'The Seoul Markets Brief';
const DESC =
  'A free email brief on Korean markets, in English. Every figure carries its source and its timestamp.';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'WebPage',
  name: TITLE,
  description: DESC,
  url: `${SITE_URL}/newsletter`,
  isPartOf: { '@id': `${SITE_URL}/#organization` },
};
---

<Base title={TITLE} description={DESC} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="page-head">
      <p class="kicker">Newsletter</p>
      <h1>{TITLE}</h1>
      <p>
        Korean market data, read in English by someone who checks the timestamp. Free, and it stays
        free — the newsletter is not a paywall funnel and there is nothing to upgrade to.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Newsletter source="newsletter" />

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What is actually in it</h2>
      <p>
        Most English-language coverage of Korean markets is a translated summary of a Korean
        headline, published a day late without saying where the number came from. We start from the
        release itself — the government dataset, the regulatory filing, the research note — and we
        say which one, and as of when.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>What moved and against what.</strong> Equities, the won, government bonds,
          commodities — with the release time of every figure, not just the figure.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The target-price scoreboard.</strong> We hold {stats.records.toLocaleString()} Korean
          brokerage research records going back to {stats.first_day}, covering {stats.brokers} houses
          and {stats.subjects.toLocaleString()} listed companies. {stats.with_target_price.toLocaleString()}
          of them carry a published target price. That lets us ask a question nobody publishes in
          English: <em>whose targets actually landed?</em>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What the data does not show.</strong> When a figure is provisional, revised, or
          simply too thin to carry a conclusion, we say so instead of writing around it.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What is not in it</h2>
      <p>
        No tips, no price targets of our own, no trade ideas. We are a data publication, not an
        adviser — nothing here is investment advice, and we hold no positions. When we report that a
        brokerage set a target, that is a fact about the brokerage, not a recommendation from us.
      </p>

      <h2>How we handle your address</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>No account.</strong> There is no password, no profile, and nothing to log into.
          One field.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>We never sell or share the list.</strong> Not to advertisers, not to partners.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>We do not log your IP</strong> when you subscribe. We store your address, the date
          you joined, and which page you came from. That is all.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>One-click unsubscribe</strong> in every issue. No confirmation loop, no
          “are you sure”.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="timing">When the first issue goes out</h2>
      <p>
        Honestly: not yet. We are still filling the archive that the brief is built on, and we would
        rather start late than start thin. Subscribing now puts you on the list for the first issue —
        you will not get anything before then, and you will not get anything else.
      </p>

      <p class="asof">
        Published by {PUBLISHER.legalName} · <a href="/about">About and methodology</a> ·
        <a href={`mailto:${PUBLISHER.email}`}>{PUBLISHER.email}</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
